<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-histogram-bins">
  <title>How Bins Shape the Picture</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Explain how bin width and starting point change the appearance of a histogram.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognize that shape, center, and spread read from a histogram are
      partly artifacts of binning.</p></li>
      <li><p>Identify histograms whose bins are too wide or too narrow to be useful.</p></li>
      <li><p>Choose a bin width appropriate to the context of the data.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>We now have two graphs that summarize the distribution of a
    quantitative variable: the dotplot and the histogram. From a dotplot we
    described the pattern in the data with statements about
    <em>shape</em>, <em>center</em>, and <em>spread</em>.</p>

    <p>We have to be more careful making the same statements from a histogram.
    A dotplot shows every individual value, so its appearance is fixed by the
    data. A histogram shows <em>grouped</em> values, and the grouping is a
    choice. Our perception of shape, center, and spread can change depending
    on how the bins are defined.</p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-three-histograms">
    <title>The same data, three ways</title>

    <p>Twenty-one students took an exam. Here are their scores:</p>

    <p><m>46</m>, <m>48</m>, <m>52</m>, <m>55</m>, <m>57</m>, <m>58</m>,
    <m>63</m>, <m>66</m>, <m>68</m>, <m>69</m>, <m>70</m>, <m>71</m>,
    <m>72</m>, <m>77</m>, <m>78</m>, <m>82</m>, <m>84</m>, <m>85</m>,
    <m>88</m>, <m>93</m>, <m>96</m></p>

    <p>Below are three histograms built from exactly these twenty-one numbers.
    Nothing about the data changed between them.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-bins-wide">
      <caption>Bin width <m>20</m>, first bin starting at <m>40</m>. The
      distribution looks symmetric with a single central peak.</caption>
      <image width="70%" xml:id="image-bins-wide" source="R-generated/exam-bins-wide">
        <description>A histogram of 21 exam scores using bins of width 20
        starting at 40. There are three bars: 6 students from 40 to 60, 9
        students from 60 to 80, and 6 students from 80 to 100. The picture is
        perfectly symmetric with a peak in the middle.</description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-bins-medium">
      <caption>Bin width <m>10</m>, first bin still starting at <m>40</m>.
      Now it appears that most students scored between <m>70</m> and
      <m>80</m>.</caption>
      <image width="70%" xml:id="image-bins-medium" source="R-generated/exam-bins-medium">
        <description>A histogram of the same 21 exam scores using bins of
        width 10 starting at 40. The six bars have heights 2, 4, 4, 5, 4, and
        2. The tallest bar covers scores from 70 to 80.</description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-bins-shifted">
      <caption>Bin width still <m>10</m>, but the first bin now starts at
      <m>45</m>. The distribution now looks slightly skewed to the right, and
      typical scores appear to fall between <m>65</m> and <m>75</m>.</caption>
      <image width="70%" xml:id="image-bins-shifted" source="R-generated/exam-bins-shifted">
        <description>A histogram of the same 21 exam scores using bins of
        width 10 starting at 45 instead of 40. The six bars have heights 3, 4,
        6, 4, 3, and 1. The tallest bar now covers scores from 65 to 75, and a
        short bar at the far right gives the distribution a slight rightward
        tail.</description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>These are not small differences.
    <xref ref="fig-bins-wide"/> is perfectly symmetric.
    <xref ref="fig-bins-shifted"/> leans right. Reading
    <xref ref="fig-bins-medium"/>, you would say most students scored in the
    <m>70</m>s. Reading <xref ref="fig-bins-shifted"/>, you would say typical
    students scored between <m>65</m> and <m>75</m>. Same twenty-one
    students.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 1 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-same-data">
      <statement correct="yes">
        <p>All three histograms above display exactly the same twenty-one exam
        scores.</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>True, and this is the point of the section. Nothing about the data
        changed. Only the bin width and the starting point of the first bin
        changed, and that was enough to alter the apparent shape of the
        distribution.</p>
      </feedback>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-why-bins-matter">
    <title>Why the grouping changes the picture</title>

    <p>Five students scored in the <m>70</m>s: <m>70</m>, <m>71</m>,
    <m>72</m>, <m>77</m>, and <m>78</m>. Follow just those five through the
    three graphs.</p>

    <p>In <xref ref="fig-bins-wide"/>, all five land in the middle bin
    together with the four students who scored <m>63</m>, <m>66</m>,
    <m>68</m>, and <m>69</m>. They disappear into a group of nine.</p>

    <p>In <xref ref="fig-bins-medium"/>, those five form a bin entirely of
    their own, because no other student scored between <m>70</m> and
    <m>80</m>. That is the highlighted bar, and it is what makes the
    <m>70</m>s look like the typical performance.</p>

    <p>In <xref ref="fig-bins-shifted"/>, the five get split: <m>70</m>,
    <m>71</m>, and <m>72</m> fall in the bin from <m>65</m> to <m>75</m>,
    while <m>77</m> and <m>78</m> fall in the bin from <m>75</m> to
    <m>85</m>. The group of five never appears as a group at all.</p>

    <p>When we change the bins, the data gets grouped differently, and the
    different grouping changes the appearance of the histogram. Nothing
    deceptive is happening. Every one of these graphs is an accurate count.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 2 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-five-students">
      <statement>
        <p>Why do the five students who scored in the <m>70</m>s form their
        own bar in <xref ref="fig-bins-medium"/> but not in
        <xref ref="fig-bins-shifted"/>?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>The shifted bins cut through the middle of the group, splitting
            it across two bars.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. The boundary at <m>75</m> falls between <m>72</m> and
            <m>77</m>, dividing a group that the other binning kept
            together.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The shifted histogram uses a wider bin, so more students are
            absorbed into each bar.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Both histograms use a bin width of <m>10</m>. Only the starting
            point changed.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Some of those students are excluded from the shifted
            histogram.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Every student appears in every histogram. All three have bars
            summing to <m>21</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The shifted histogram sorts the scores differently before
            binning.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Binning does not depend on the order of the data. Each value is
            simply placed into whichever interval contains it.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-choosing-bins">
    <title>Choosing a bin width</title>

    <p>If three defensible choices give three different pictures, which one
    should we use? There is no formula, but there are two failure modes to
    avoid.</p>

    <assemblage xml:id="asm-bin-advice">
      <title>Two ways to go wrong</title>
      <p><em>Bins too wide.</em> Grouping the data into only a few bins
      produces a <q>skyscraper</q><mdash/>a couple of towering bars that say
      almost nothing about variability within the group.</p>
      <p><em>Bins too narrow.</em> Grouping the data into very many bins
      produces a <q>pancake</q><mdash/>a long row of bars one or two units
      tall, where random gaps look like real features and no pattern is
      visible.</p>
      <p>Between those extremes, prefer a bin width that means something in
      context.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>That last point is what settles the exam-score example. Of the three
    graphs, <xref ref="fig-bins-medium"/> is the most useful summary<mdash/>not
    because <m>10</m>-point bins are mathematically optimal, but because
    <m>10</m>-point intervals starting at round numbers correspond to letter
    grades. The bins carry meaning the reader already understands.</p>

    <p>A good practice is to look at more than one binning before describing a
    distribution, and to be suspicious of any feature that appears under one
    choice and vanishes under another.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 3 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-skyscraper">
      <statement>
        <p>A student builds a histogram of <m>400</m> commute times using just
        two bins. What is the problem?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>Two bins hide almost all the variability<mdash/>the graph is a
            <q>skyscraper</q> that says little more than a single count
            would.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. With bins that wide, individuals with very different
            commute times get lumped together, and the shape of the
            distribution is invisible.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Histograms are required to have at least five bins.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>There is no such rule. The guidance in
            <xref ref="asm-bin-advice" text="title"/> is about usefulness, not
            a minimum count.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The bar heights will not add up to <m>400</m>.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>They will. Every individual lands in exactly one bin no matter
            how wide the bins are.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Two bins will make the distribution look skewed when it is
            symmetric.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The real problem is more basic: with two bars there is
            essentially no shape to read at all, skewed or otherwise.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 4 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-pancake">
      <statement>
        <p>A researcher graphs the ages of <m>60</m> survey respondents using
        a bin width of one year, producing about <m>50</m> bars that are
        mostly <m>0</m>, <m>1</m>, or <m>2</m> units tall. What should she
        do?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>Widen the bins, so the overall pattern becomes visible.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. This is the <q>pancake</q> problem. With <m>60</m>
            values spread over <m>50</m> bins, the bar heights are mostly
            noise.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Narrow the bins further, to show even more detail.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That makes it worse. The graph already shows more detail than
            <m>60</m> observations can support.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Remove the bins with a count of zero.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Deleting empty bins would break the numeric scale on the
            horizontal axis and misrepresent where the gaps in the data
            are.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Collect more data, since histograms require large samples.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>More data would help, but it is not necessary. A sensible bin
            width will give a readable histogram of these <m>60</m>
            values.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 5: short answer, recorded -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-defend-bins">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose you are graphing the annual household incomes of families
        in a county, where values range from near <m>\$0</m> to about
        <m>\$400{,}000</m>. Propose a bin width, and give one sentence
        defending it. What makes your choice meaningful to a reader?</p>
        <response/>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Many answers are defensible. A bin width of
        <m>\$25{,}000</m> gives about sixteen bars across the range<mdash/>enough
        to show shape without becoming a pancake<mdash/>and the boundaries fall
        at round figures that readers can hold in their heads.</p>
        <p>A bin width of <m>\$10{,}000</m> would give forty bars, which is
        probably too many unless the county is large. A bin width of
        <m>\$100{,}000</m> would give four, which is a skyscraper.</p>
        <p>The strongest answers do what the exam-score example did: tie the
        bin boundaries to something the audience already recognizes, such as
        federal poverty thresholds or tax brackets.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <conclusion>
    <title>Summary</title>
    <p>Descriptions of shape, center, and spread read from a histogram are
    affected by how the bins are defined. Changing the bin width, or keeping
    the width and shifting the starting point, regroups the data and can
    change the apparent shape of the distribution.</p>
    <p>Avoid bins so wide that the graph becomes a <q>skyscraper</q> hiding all
    variability, and bins so narrow that it becomes a <q>pancake</q> showing
    only noise. When a bin width has meaning in context, prefer it.</p>
    <p>A dotplot does not have this problem, because it shows every individual
    value. That is the trade: the histogram handles large data sets, and pays
    for it with a dependence on the binning choice.</p>
    <p>Adapted from <pubtitle>Concepts in Statistics</pubtitle>
    (SUNY / Lumen Learning), CC BY 4.0.</p>
  </conclusion>
</section>